      "text": "We start with the given equation:\n\\[x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34.\\]\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation to bring all terms to one side:\n\\[x^2 - 10x + y^2 + 6y = -34.\\]\n\nNext, we complete the square for both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms. For the \\(x\\) terms, we take half of \\(-10\\), square it, and add and subtract this value inside the equation:\n\\[x^2 - 10x = (x - 5)^2 - 25.\\]\n\nFor the \\(y\\) terms, we take half of \\(6\\), square it, and add and subtract this value inside the equation:\n\\[y^2 + 6y = (y + 3)^2 - 9.\\]\n\nSubstituting these completed squares back into the equation, we get:\n\\[(x - 5)^2 - 25 + (y + 3)^2 - 9 = -34.\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we have:\n\\[(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 - 34 = -34.\\]\n\nAdding 34 to both sides of the equation, we get:\n\\[(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0.\\]\n\nSince the sum of two squares is zero, each square must be zero. Therefore, we have:\n\\[(x - 5)^2 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (y + 3)^2 = 0.\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[x - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y + 3 = 0.\\]\n\nSo, we find:\n\\[x = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = -3.\\]\n\nAdding these values together, we get:\n\\[x + y = 5 + (-3) = 2.\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{2}.\\]",
