      "text": "Given the equation \\( x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34 \\), we start by rearranging all terms to one side of the equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 - 10x + 6y + 34 = 0\n\\]\n\nNext, we will complete the square for both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). Starting with the \\(x\\)-terms:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x \\quad \\text{can be rewritten as} \\quad (x-5)^2 - 25\n\\]\n\nNow, for the \\(y\\)-terms:\n\n\\[\ny^2 + 6y \\quad \\text{can be rewritten as} \\quad (y+3)^2 - 9\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these completed squares back into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n(x-5)^2 - 25 + (y+3)^2 - 9 + 34 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the constants on the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n(x-5)^2 + (y+3)^2 - 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nAdding 2 to both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\n(x-5)^2 + (y+3)^2 = 2\n\\]\n\nThis is the equation of a circle with center \\((5, -3)\\) and radius \\(\\sqrt{2}\\). \n\nTo find \\(x + y\\), we need to determine the coordinates of the center of the circle and the corresponding values of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) that satisfy the equation. However, we notice that the center \\((5, -3)\\) does not directly give us \\(x + y\\). Instead, we focus on the symmetry and properties of the circle.\n\nThe symmetry of the circle suggests that if we move from the center \\((5, -3)\\), the sum \\(x + y\\) will be symmetric around the origin. To find \\(x + y\\), we can use the fact that the circle's equation is symmetric and the center's coordinates help us understand the relationship.\n\nGiven the circle's radius and position, we can infer that the sum \\(x + y\\) will be the same as the center's \\(x\\)-coordinate plus the \\(y\\)-coordinate. Therefore:\n\n\\[\nx + y = 5 + (-3) = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
