    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "\\[ x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34 \\implies x^2 - 10x + y^2 + 6y = -34 \\]\n\\[ \\implies (x^2 - 10x + 25) + (y^2 + 6y + 9) = -34 + 25 + 9 \\]\n\\[ \\implies (x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0 \\]\n\\[ \\implies (x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0 \\implies ((x-5),(y+3)) = (0,0) \\]\n\\[ \\implies x - 5 = 0, y + 3 = 0 \\]\n\\[ \\implies x = 5, y = -3 \\]\n\\[ \\therefore x + y = 5 + (-3) = 2 \\boxed{2} \\]\n\nThe sides of a triangle in geometric progression are 2, $x$, and 18. Find $x$.\n\n1. Let the sides of the triangle in geometric progression be \\( a = 2 \\), \\( b = x \\), and \\( c = 18 \\).\n\n2. In a geometric progression, the ratio between consecutive terms is constant. Therefore, the common ratio \\( r \\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   r = \\sqrt{\\frac{b}{a}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}\n   \\]\n   and also by:\n   \\[\n   r = \\sqrt{\\frac{c}{b}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{18}{x}}\n   \\]\n\n3. Equating the two expressions for the common ratio \\( r \\):\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{18}{x}}\n   \\]\n\n4. Squaring both sides to eliminate the square roots:\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}} \\right)^2 = \\left( \\sqrt{\\frac{18}{x}} \\right)^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x}{2} = \\frac{18}{x}\n   \\]\n\n5. Cross-multiplying to solve for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   x \\cdot x = 18 \\cdot 2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x^2 = 36\n   \\]\n\n6. Taking the square root of both sides:\n   \\[\n   x = \\sqrt{36}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 6 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -6\n   \\]\n\n7. Since \\( x \\) represents a side length of a triangle, it must be positive. Therefore:\n   \\[\n   x = 6\n   \\]\n\nThe final answer is \\( \\boxed{6} \\).",
